  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA mid-level ridge will continue to control our weather pattern \nthrough early next week resulting in hot and dry conditions for the \nCWA. However, the aforementioned ridge's eastern flank will weaken \ndue to a strengthening trough in the Ohio Valley resulting in \nincreased moisture filtering into the area. Model guidance is \nsupportive of this as PWAT values are over 2\", with isolated spots \napproaching 2.2\". In addition, vorticity in the mid-levels (500 mb) \nwill support the development of showers and thunderstorms over \nCentral Texas this afternoon. Some guidance hints at a broken line \nof showers entering our northern counties tonight. However, this \nline looks to be in its decaying stage by the time it reaches our \narea so will maintain only 15-20% rain chances. Weak low-lvl \nconvergence and moisture pooling nearly the largely-washed out \nboundary will maintain low-end rain chances through the day on \nSunday before drier air moves in for the upcoming workweek and rain \nchances return to near zero.\n\nDue to the influence of the aforementioned mid-level ridge, hot \ntemperatures will prevail through the first week of August. Areas \neast of Highway 77 have a high chance of highs exceeding 100F \nthrough early next week. Today, heat index values will likely exceed \n110F over much of the Coastal Plains (even approaching 115 in \nspots), and therefore a heat advisory has been issued for that \nregion. Although there should be enough mixing of drier air towards \nthe surface over the Brush Country/Rio Grande Plains to keep that \nregion below criteria it will still be quite hot with a largely \nmajor (level 3/4) heat risk. This drier air will persist over the \nBrush Country and Rio Grande Plains through most of the period with \nminimum RH levels dropping below 30% each afternoon. This, coupled \nwith wind gusts between 10-15 kts will allow for an elevated fire \nrisk into early next week.",
